      "cooked": "<p>The card reads,</p><blockquote><p><span data-sheets-userformat=\"[null,null,15040,null,null,null,null,null,null,0,1,null,0,null,[null,2,0],&quot;calibri,arial,sans,sans-serif&quot;,11]\" data-sheets-value=\"[null,2,&quot;Whenever The Scholar would be dealt Damage, reduce that Damage to 0.\\nAt the start of your turn, The Scholar regains 2 HP and destroys this card.&quot;]\">Whenever The Scholar would be dealt Damage, reduce that Damage to 0.\" </span></p></blockquote><p><span data-sheets-userformat=\"[null,null,15040,null,null,null,null,null,null,0,1,null,0,null,[null,2,0],&quot;calibri,arial,sans,sans-serif&quot;,11]\" data-sheets-value=\"[null,2,&quot;Whenever The Scholar would be dealt Damage, reduce that Damage to 0.\\nAt the start of your turn, The Scholar regains 2 HP and destroys this card.&quot;]\">So since it doesn't say \"immune to damage,\" does this mean that irreducible damage will still get through normally? Hasn't come up in any of my games yet (thus far, the only time we've used him is against the Ennead, where this card and an out of turn Alchemical Redirection bought us a round of safety), but I want to make sure for future games.</span></p>",
