      "raw": "<p>The card reads,</p><blockquote><p><span data-sheets-userformat=\"[null,null,15040,null,null,null,null,null,null,0,1,null,0,null,[null,2,0],&quot;calibri,arial,sans,sans-serif&quot;,11]\" data-sheets-value=\"[null,2,&quot;Whenever The Scholar would be dealt Damage, reduce that Damage to 0.\\nAt the start of your turn, The Scholar regains 2 HP and destroys this card.&quot;]\" style=\"font-size:110%;font-family:calibri,arial,sans,sans-serif;color:#000000;text-align:left;\">Whenever The Scholar would be dealt Damage, reduce that Damage to 0.&quot; </span></p></blockquote><p><span data-sheets-userformat=\"[null,null,15040,null,null,null,null,null,null,0,1,null,0,null,[null,2,0],&quot;calibri,arial,sans,sans-serif&quot;,11]\" data-sheets-value=\"[null,2,&quot;Whenever The Scholar would be dealt Damage, reduce that Damage to 0.\\nAt the start of your turn, The Scholar regains 2 HP and destroys this card.&quot;]\" style=\"font-size:110%;font-family:calibri,arial,sans,sans-serif;color:#000000;text-align:left;\">So since it doesn&#39;t say &quot;immune to damage,&quot; does this mean that irreducible damage will still get through normally? Hasn&#39;t come up in any of my games yet (thus far, the only time we&#39;ve used him is against the Ennead, where this card and an out of turn Alchemical Redirection bought us a round of safety), but I want to make sure for future games.</span></p>",
